  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2c/X-Men_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first 3 pages are spent showing the various X-Men making their way to the School for Gifted Youngsters (apparently from New Haven, CT) after receiving a mental summons from Professor X, who then shows them a mental image of the \"Vanisher\" who robs a bank and then vanishes.&nbsp; He suspects he's a mutant like them and proceeds to have them step into \"The Danger Room,\" where they practice their skills until the middle of page 10.\n\nBut while the Professor tests his students...\n\n\"Meanwhile,\" the Vanisher appears inside the Pentagon and proceeds to proclaim that he is indeed a mutant, even using the \"Homo Superior\" lingo.&nbsp; He is only there to gloat, declaring that he will be returning \"within a few days\" to steal some secret defense plans.&nbsp; (He is already recognized by one of the men inside the Pentagon.)\n\nAfter the newspapers cover the story, we next see the Vanisher in his lair, now surrounded by low-level crooks, who seem happy to become his lackeys.&nbsp; The narration indicates that there has been enough time for his exploits to have been published in newspapers.\n\n\"Meanwhile,\" back in the Danger Room, the lesson continues until a pre-appointed time, where we learn that he has an agreement with FBI Agent Fred Duncan.&nbsp; It's time to mentally contact him with the help of a \"psionic headband.\"&nbsp; It's apparently been a \"few days\" now, and the people in Washington DC are expecting the Vanisher's attack at any moment.\n\nThe X-Men immediately head off to Washington DC, on board a \"military air taxi.\"&nbsp; Sure enough, the Vanisher strikes at the Pentagon, stealing the plans, only to be confronted by the X-Men.&nbsp; They engage, during which time Angel tells Jean to use \"Maneuver eighteen!\"&nbsp; During the fight, the Vanisher seems surprised to learn that the X-Men are mutants also.&nbsp; He gasses Jean and vanishes with the plans.&nbsp; The newspapers report their failure the next day \"X-MEN FAIL IN ATTEMPT TO HALT THEFT!\"\n\nThe Vanisher then demands 10 million dollars to not turn over the plans to the Communists.&nbsp; People everywhere call the X-Men a bunch of phonies.&nbsp; Professor X now has to step in to help his team.&nbsp; The next day outside the White House, the X-Men and the Vanisher (with his gang of lackeys) face off.&nbsp; He's come to collect his 10 million.&nbsp; But the Professor is now with them, and after a single creepy stare, the Vanisher suddenly cannot control his ability, and he's even forgotten who he is!\n\nWith the Vanisher subdued, the X-Men make quick work of the flunkies, and all is well again!",
  "grade_one_liner": "A tangle of timing errors, logic gaps, and vanished common sense.\nOnly the Danger Room shows up ready to work.&nbsp; The rest of the issue doesn't.",
  "subtitle": "The Case of the Vanished Sense of Time",
  "review_text": "Similar to my <i>Avengers</i> 2 ([LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2]) review, this one was so fraught with problems from the very start that even outlining the Plot Summary became a chore.&nbsp; Again, rather than litter it with [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"What the--?\"] reactions, I instead present a list of inconsistencies here:\n\n[HANG0] * The splash page shows the Beast wearing gloves (and then never again).&nbsp; The Beast <i>never</i> wears gloves, as you can see in the first issue (and indeed, even on page 2 of <i>this very issue</i>).&nbsp; This blunder was included in the <i>Marvel No-Prize</i> book published in the 1980s.\n\n[HANG0] * The X-Men are shown rushing to get to Westchester County and back to the School for Gifted Youngsters.&nbsp; The Beast even indicates that he's in New Haven (which is in Connecticut) and catches the \"commuter special\" to make his way.&nbsp; He implies, in fact, they are all <i>together</i> in New Haven.\n[BR]\n[BR] But... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=why is it that any of them had to make their way to the school at all?]&nbsp; The first issue suggested they were <i>attending</i> the boarding school, so why are they scattered across New England like they're commuting to a day job?\n\n[HANG0] * The girls who mob the Angel on page 2 seem to already know who he is, which <i>alone</i> presents a problem.&nbsp; And when Jean mentally moves the girls aside, the Angel warns her not to strain her \"[SPAN: severity=error; text=teleportation] powers\" (odd, considering that she has [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=telekinetic]<i> </i>powers).&nbsp; (The error is repeated on page 7.&nbsp; It's almost impressive how consistently wrong it manages to be.)\n\n[HANG0] * Cyclops is <i>also</i> recognized by workers whom he saves from a falling brick wall.&nbsp; Curious that people seem aware of the X-Men already.&nbsp; He and Iceman catch a ride back to the school on an ice cream truck, while Angel flies there under his own power, with Jean in tow.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 10 shows the Vanisher using the same \"Homo Superior\" terminology as Magneto did in the first issue.&nbsp; Is this simply a coincidence, or did Magneto publish a research paper on the topic?\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOnce the X-Men have returned to the school, the Professor informs them about the Vanisher and then ushers them into the Danger Room to begin a training session.\n\n\"Meanwhile,\" the Vanisher appears inside the Pentagon and announces that he will return within \"a few days\" to steal some defense plans...\n[ASSET: center | StealYourPlans | width=67%]\n\nThe very next panel then shows him at his secret lair, surrounded by low-level crooks.&nbsp; The narration explicitly states that this happens \"later, after reading of his incredible exploits in the newspapers,\" which means that sufficient time has gone by to make this <i>possible</i>.\n[ASSET: center | HiddenLair | width=67%]\nThe Vanisher actually <i>tells</i> these crooks that they shall become his \"lackeys.\"\n[BR] And their response is to say \"Three cheers for the <i>Vanisher</i>!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Wow!</i>  This gives a whole new <i>meaning</i> to \"low-level crooks!\")]\n\nBut despite the fact that the newspapers have already published the story of the Vanisher's exploits at the Pentagon, Professor X has not ceased his training of the X-Men, because they're still in the Danger Room (see page 12, where the term \"Meanwhile\" is used in the narrative)!\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsAlmostTime | width=16%] When he finally <i>does</i> end the session and mentally reaches out to Fred Duncan to learn about the latest Vanisher developments, we learn that people in Washington DC \"are expecting his attack at any moment!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] This makes it clear that the \"few days\" are now up.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Which, in turn, makes the length of the Danger Room session retroactively absurd.&nbsp; <b><i>Whoa!</i></b>)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[HANG0] * Page 13 shows the X-Men aboard a \"military air taxi\" in order to fly to Washington DC.&nbsp; But on page 12 of the first issue ([LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1]), they boarded a \"remote-control private jet\" to fly all the way to Florida in order to face off against Magneto (and, indeed, return in it, as shown on the issue's final panel).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why did they suddenly need a military air taxi if they already had a private jet at their disposal?]&nbsp; It's as if the private jet fell into a continuity pothole between issues.\n\n[ASSET: right | WhyNotUseASafe | width=12%] I actually laughed when I reached page 14 and saw the \"important continental defense plans\" sitting on a desk while surrounded by four soldiers with guns.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Oh sure</i>!&nbsp; Just lay the plans out in plain view, why don't you?]\n[BR]\n[BR] Did <i>no one </i>bother to ask if a <b><i>locked safe</i></b><i> </i>might have been a better choice?&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(<i>Oh my!</i>  Our national security at work!)]\n\n[HANG0] * The Vanisher recognizes the X-Men at the bottom of page 14, but page 16 implies that he's unaware they are mutants like himself.\n\n[HANG0] * The Professor shows his students some footage he says was taken by a slow-motion camera capable of \"20,000 frames a second\" (page 19).&nbsp; But as this article notes: [CITATION: https://petapixel.com/2013/08/20/blast-from-the-past-18000fps-high-speed-photography-in-the-1960s], the maximum speed available at the time was 18,000fps.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Yes, I </i><b>know</b> it's a comic book, but I thought that would be fun to toss that in!)]\n[BR]\n[BR] Technical quibbles aside, the real problem is narrative, in that [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>none</i> of this information is necessary to the plot!]&nbsp; The footage is shown, shared with the folks at the White House for them to intently study, but when the final confrontation takes place, the Professor merely assaults the Vanisher mentally, making him forget who he is and how to use his powers.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=So what was the point of the footage in the first place?]\n\nThe last couple of pages show the X-Men easily taking down the low-level crooks.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nGiven the host of... <i>er...</i> <i>questionable</i> decision-making in the plot, I am forced to conclude that this was about as weak a second issue as the aforementioned [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2].&nbsp; There were <i>so many pages</i> wasted just showing the X-Men rushing to get back to the school or fighting amongst themselves in a story that already struggled to be relevant.\n\nFurthermore, [SPAN: severity=info; text=no information was provided about the Vanisher's past.]&nbsp; It's as if he materialized fully formed just to steal paperwork.&nbsp; (But to be fair, the lack of backstory could be said of Magneto in the <i>previous</i> issue.)\n\nBut to get to the most <i>damning</i> of the many problems I enumerated:&nbsp; I complained a couple of reviews ago that this month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2] had a timing problem, in that the entire episode couldn't have taken place in more than an hour or so.&nbsp; In <i>this</i> issue, we have the <b><i>opposite</i></b> problem, where it seems the Professor's Danger Room session (that spans pages 7-12) takes place over an elapsed time of a \"few days.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=<i>Boy</i>, that Professor is downright <b>brutal</b>!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Either that, or time itself is afraid to contradict him.)]\n\nObviously, the co-creators once again lost track of time, making this a [SPAN: severity=error; text=blatant error] and <i>clearly</i> not what was intended.&nbsp; It could have been easily corrected by reversing just a couple of scenes.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Oh well...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | DangerRoom | width=13%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=On the positive side,] the establishment of the Danger Room at least gives the X-Men a place where they can showcase the use of their powers.&nbsp; While it's a nice idea (especially in a \"school\" environment), you don't see the Fantastic Four doing anything of the sort (though perhaps they <i>should</i>).&nbsp; But more to the point, it provides Lee and Kirby with a built-in way to pad the page count under the guise of \"training.\"\n\nAs for this story, in the end, the Professor shows up and takes care of the problem single-handedly.\n[BR] This leads to a couple of very obvious questions:\n[HANG] * Why did he even need the X-Men if he could have dealt with the Vanisher by himself all <i>along</i>?\n[HANG] * Was his physical presence actually required?&nbsp; Is there perhaps some sort of distance limitation to his powers?\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyLook | width=12%] The answer to the first question is likely provided on page 18, where the Professor indicates to them that he was letting them get experience.\n[BR]\n[BR] And if for no other reason than Jack Kirby got to draw this [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=downright creepy look] on the face of the Professor, I guess it's okay that he showed up in person!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Holy cow!</i>)]\n\nYes, it clearly establishes Professor X as a significant presence, both in that he can handle himself <i>and</i> that he has friendly contacts inside the FBI.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=That's no small thing.]&nbsp; And <i>yes</i>,<i> </i>this gives him a bit of motivation regarding the formation of a team dedicated to the identification and training of mutants, [SPAN: severity=success; text=so that's all well and good.]\n\nBut this issue did very little to advance the <i>team</i>.&nbsp; They seemed completely inept (except for rounding up a handful of lowlifes).&nbsp; It's hard to imagine anyone reading this and thinking, \"[SPAN: severity=info; text=Yes, these are the future pillars of a franchise.]\"&nbsp; At this stage, they're barely a functional study group.\n\nAs for the magazine itself, this issue seemed as devoid of life as the team is of competence -- a shaky outing for a title still trying to find its footing.",
  "ownership": "I did own a VG copy of this book years ago, which I bought on Feb 19, 2000 for $183.75 (but not part of the \"2000 collection\"), but it was sold that same year, <i>well</i> before my full 2018 inventory, so I did not count it as being \"owned\" anymore.&nbsp; I have no record of how much I got for it.",
  "highlight": "The X-Men corral the Vanisher after a Danger Room session that lasts longer than the plot."
}